name: pipes and counting
boss: PipeWrench
flavor: Nothing here stands alone. It all flows through me.
brief: A pipe wires one command's stdout into the next one's stdin. That is
brief: the whole idea, and it is why this system needs so few programs: you
brief: are not given a tool for every job, you are given parts to build one.
brief:
brief: sort orders, uniq collapses neighbours, wc counts. uniq only sees the
brief: line before it, so it can only remove duplicates that are already
brief: adjacent -- which is why sort comes first, every time.
---
q: count the lines in @log@
a: wc -l < @log@
a: wc -l @log@
out: 10
ref: wc
teach: wc -l
---
q: count the words in @txt@
a: wc -w < @txt@
a: wc -w @txt@
out: [0-9]
ref: wc
teach: wc -w
---
q: count the bytes in @txt@
a: wc -c < @txt@
a: wc -c @txt@
out: [0-9]
ref: wc
teach: wc -c
---
q: count how many entries are in the current directory
a: ls | wc -l
out: [0-9]
ref: wc
teach: wc -l
---
q: sort the lines of @txt@ alphabetically
a: sort @txt@
out: ^ask why the fan
ref: sort
teach: sort
---
q: sort @txt@ in reverse alphabetical order
a: sort -r @txt@
out: ^verify the root hash
ref: sort
teach: sort -r
---
q: print the lines of @txt@ with duplicates removed, using one command
a: sort -u @txt@
out: ask why the fan
ref: sort
teach: sort -u
---
q: print the lines of @txt@ with duplicates removed, using sort and uniq
a: sort @txt@ | uniq
out: ask why the fan
ref: uniq
teach: uniq
hint: uniq only collapses ADJACENT duplicates, so sort has to go first
---
q: show each distinct line of @txt@ with a count of how many times it appears
a: sort @txt@ | uniq -c
out: 2 rotate the logs
ref: uniq
teach: uniq -c
---
q: show only the lines of @txt@ that appear more than once
a: sort @txt@ | uniq -d
out: rotate the logs
ref: uniq
teach: uniq -d
---
q: show only the lines of @txt@ that appear exactly once
a: sort @txt@ | uniq -u
out: ask why the fan
ref: uniq
teach: uniq -u
---
q: sort @csv@ by its third comma-separated field
a: sort -t, -k3 @csv@
out: down
ref: sort
teach: sort -t
hint: -t sets the separator, -k picks the field
---
q: sort @csv@ by the fourth field, numerically, largest first
a: sort -t, -k4 -rn @csv@
a: sort -t, -k4rn @csv@
out: 42
ref: sort
teach: sort -k
---
q: sort @txt@ ignoring the difference between upper and lower case
a: sort -f @txt@
out: ask why the fan
ref: sort
teach: sort -f
---
q: check whether @txt@ is already sorted, without printing it
a: sort -c @txt@
ref: sort
teach: sort -c
hint: it says nothing when sorted, and names the first bad line when not
---
q: sort @csv@ by human-readable sizes like 2K and 1M, smallest first
a: sort -h @csv@
out: .
ref: sort
teach: sort -h
---
q: sort @txt@ ignoring case differences when comparing
a: sort -i @txt@
out: .
ref: sort
teach: sort -i
---
q: sort @txt@ writing the result back over the file itself
a: sort -o sorted.txt @txt@
state: exists:sorted.txt
ref: sort
teach: sort -o
---
q: sort a list of version numbers so 1.10 comes after 1.9
a: sort -V @txt@
out: .
ref: sort
teach: sort -V
---
q: collapse duplicate lines ignoring case
a: sort -f @txt@ | uniq -i
out: .
ref: uniq
teach: uniq -i
---
q: collapse duplicates comparing only the first 6 characters of each line
a: sort @txt@ | uniq -w 6
out: .
ref: uniq
teach: uniq -w
---
q: find the length of the longest line in @log@
a: wc -L @log@
out: [0-9]
ref: wc
teach: wc -L
